rately lower than conventional processing according to the entity description. Slab heating is directly linked to both the slabs and the specified 1150 C condition. [Data: Entities (1167, 1251, 1193, 1247); Relationships (1705, 1769, 1770)]\\n\\nThe evidence supports treating heating temperature as a controlled upstream parameter that conditions subsequent roughing and finishing operations.\"},{\"summary\":\"Roughing establishes the intermediate product and cooling sequence\",\"explanation\":\"Roughing rolling takes place in the roughing stand, potentially at high speed, and produces a semi-finished product with a thickness of 50 ММ before finishing rolling. After high-speed roughing, the slab or rolled stock may remain in a queue and cool before reaching the required finishing-stand entry temperature. [Data: Entities (1181, 1188, 1236, 1226, 1216, 1253); Relationships (1706, 1771, 1740, 1772)]\\n\\nThis indicates that roughing speed, intermediate thickness, and queue cooling are coordinated elements of the production route.\"},{\"summary\":\"Finishing stand operation is continuous and temperature-constrained\",\"explanation\":\"The finishing stand is the continuous rolling stand used to complete the required deformation. Finishing-stand deformation must be completed above Ar₃, with part of the deformation occurring in the lower austenitic region and the intercritical temperature interval. The specified finishing temperature is approximately 710–740 C. [Data: Entities (1189, 1182, 1201, 1222, 1252); Relationships (1707, 1708, 1742, 1749, 1775)]\\n\\nThe data also links queue cooling with continuous finishing-stand operation, showing that upstream cooling and downstream rolling must be synchronized to maintain the required entry and deformation conditions.\"},{\"summary\":\"Intercritical deformation trades toughness and plasticity for strength\",\"explanation\":\"Deformation in the intercritical temperature interval is described as increasing strength characteristics while decreasing plasticity and toughness. The process community therefore includes an explicit materials-property tradeoff. [Data: Entities (1196, 1197, 1182); Relationships (1720, 1721, 1708)]\\n\\nThe supplied controls include a low-carbon condition, which can partly compensate for the decrease in plasticity, and a sulfur limit of up to 0,005%, also identified as a quality measure. [Data: Entities (1202, 1203, 1195); Relationships (1723, 1724)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Finishing reductions are specified for deformation and flatness\",\"explanation\":\"Each finishing-rolling pass applies a 15–18% reduction, while the final three passes apply 10% reduction each. The latter practice is explicitly associated with ensuring flatness. [Data: Entities (1238, 1254, 1255, 1257); Relationships (1777, 1779)]\\n\\nThese parameters show that the finishing schedule is not defined only by temperature; it also uses pass-by-pass reduction targets to control the resulting product characteristics.\"},{\"summary\":\"UOVT provides an alternative accelerated-cooling route\",\"explanation\":\"Accelerated-cooling controlled rolling is described as an alternative route at the 3600 mill. In this route, rolled stock is transported between the finishing stand and the UOVT installation, where accelerated cooling is performed. [Data: Entities (1234, 1249, 1268, 1240, 1239); Relationships (1782, 1804, 1783, 1784)]\\n\\nThe evidence establishes UOVT as an integrated process installation and transportation step, but it does not specify cooling rates, resulting grades, equipment capacity, or operational risks.\"},{\"summary\":\"Metallurgical quality controls support continuously cast feedstock\",\"explanation\":\"Continuous casting produces the continuously cast metal used in the controlled-rolling discussion. Quality-improvement measures include low carbon content, sulfur control, vacuum treatment, and other metallurgical refining techniques. Vacuum treatment is specifically identified as a measure for improving the quality of continuously cast metal used in the process. [Data: Entities (1219, 1220, 1223, 1204); Relationships (1750, 1751, 1725)]\\n\\nThe supplied information supports a quality-oriented process design, but provides no test results, compliance certifications, product standards, or independent assessment of performance.\"}],\"rating\":7.0,\"rating_explanation\":\"The community has high technical importance because it represents an interconnected industrial steel-production route with tightly controlled thermal, deformation, quality, and cooling parameters, while no evidence is provided of legal, reputational, or incident-related severity.\"}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1790706828, "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1457, "prompt_tokens": 4587, "total_tokens": 6044, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 85, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 4584, "cached_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_tokens": 4584}}, "formatted_response": null, "obfuscation": "bl4NCr7MaHLq8P", "content": "{\"title\":\"3600 Mill Controlled-Rolling Process and UOVT Cooling\",\"summary\":\"The community describes an integrated steel controlled-rolling route centered on slabs, reheating furnaces, roughing and finishing stands, and optional accelerated cooling through the UOVT installation. Slabs are heated to approximately 1150 C, rough-rolled into a 50 ММ semi-finished product, cooled in a queue as needed, and continuously processed in the finishing stand at 710–740 C. The process uses defined reductions, temperature thresholds, metallurgical quality contro